; Every node's span, read back out of the source it points at.
;
; `span_dump` prints one line per node: the node's kind and the text between
; its span's two positions. `sst_dump` does the same over the desugared tree
; and prints each node's variant as well, so its expectations show the rewrites
; of docs/desugaring.md having happened and not merely that spans survived
; them.
;
; What these are for: a span is only ever wrong by pointing somewhere, and the
; rest of the suite cannot see where. The acceptance tests ask whether a file
; parses, and the grouping tests ask which shape it parsed to -- neither reads
; a position. So a grammar change that widens a span to swallow a binder, or
; narrows one past its own bracket, passes everything and is noticed only in
; review, if at all.
;
; A failing diff is not by itself a bug: a change to the grammar, the fixture
; or the lowering is expected to move these. Read the diff, satisfy yourself
; that each moved span still covers what its node stands for and that each
; rewrite still produces the shape it should, and promote it with
; `dune promote`.
;
; Three fixtures. `main.zn` is the one the parser suite already reaches for,
; and it covers the breadth of the grammar. `utf8.zn` covers the one input
; class it does not: spans are byte offsets, and while a file is ASCII a
; code-point offset is indistinguishable from a byte one. They diverge at the
; first multibyte character and every span after it shifts -- which is exactly
; the bug found on #86, after the suite had been green through it.
;
; `desugar.zn` is the third, and unlike the other two it was written for these
; tests: every rewrite in docs/desugaring.md 2 appears in it at least once.
; The other two do not manage that -- main.zn reaches only two of the five
; derived operators, so three of them had no expectation anywhere.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  main.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/main.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff main.spans main.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  utf8.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/utf8.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff utf8.spans utf8.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.spans desugar.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  main.sst.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/main.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff main.sst.spans main.sst.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  utf8.sst.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/utf8.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff utf8.sst.spans utf8.sst.spans.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.sst.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/sst_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.sst.spans desugar.sst.spans.actual)))

; The two structure renderings, which name every field rather than printing a
; span. `lib/cst/to_tree_graph.ml` had no test of any kind before this -- five
; hundred lines reached only by the compiler binary -- and its SST counterpart
; would have inherited that. Rendering one fixture through each covers both.
;
; Reading them side by side is also the clearest statement of what the
; desugaring does: the SST rendering is the CST one with `parenthized`,
; `ret_shorthand`, `var_decl_shorthand`, `trailing` and one of the two call
; groups gone.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.cst.tree.actual
  (run %{exe:../bin/compiler/main.exe} --cst %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.cst.tree desugar.cst.tree.actual)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  desugar.sst.tree.actual
  (run %{exe:../bin/compiler/main.exe} --sst %{dep:../test-parser/desugar.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff desugar.sst.tree desugar.sst.tree.actual)))
